# storysync — Storybook to Figma

Read components from Storybook MCP and recreate them in Figma MCP as a visually accurate component library, with design token foundations.

## Requirements

- Storybook dev server running with `@storybook/addon-mcp` (Vite-based Storybook 10.1+, Node 18+; `snap` needs Node 20+)
- Storybook MCP in `.cursor/mcp.json`: `{ "mcpServers": { "storybook": { "url": "http://localhost:6006/mcp" } } }`
- Figma MCP: `/add-plugin figma` in Agent chat (or a `"figma": { "url": "https://mcp.figma.com/mcp" }` entry in `.cursor/mcp.json`), and sign in to Figma when prompted
- Figma Full seat (Dev seats are read-only)
- storysync 0.4.0 or later, run as `npx storysync` — 0.3.0 has no token `px`, component `id`, `--exclude-props` or `--merge`, and 0.2.0 and earlier have no `snap` or `verify`
- If storysync says Storybook has no MCP endpoint (`answered HTTP 404`, with ``Run `storysync init`, then restart Storybook``), ask the user to run `npx storysync init`, which asks before each change it makes, and then to restart Storybook. If its install fails, it prints the command to run by hand after `Run manually:`: pass that line on rather than retrying.
- The CLI's own `storysync diff` can't sign in to Figma, so against `https://mcp.figma.com/mcp` it fails with HTTP 401 by design. Audit with the Audit section below instead, through `use_figma`, and don't retry it.

## Running storysync from Cursor

Cursor runs the agent's terminal commands in a sandbox when it can, and the sandbox blocks loopback addresses (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `::1`) by default. `list`, `map`, `inspect` and `snap` all talk to Storybook on `localhost`, and `snap` also launches a browser, so request full permissions to run them outside the sandbox. If one of them fails to reach Storybook from inside the sandbox, re-run it with full permissions before telling the user Storybook is down. `tokens`, `figma code`, `figma next` and `verify` read and write only local files. `figma upload` posts pictures to the upload URLs Figma's `upload_assets` hands out, so it needs network access: run it outside the sandbox too.

## Tokens

Before syncing components, extract design tokens from the project and create Figma variable collections. This ensures components can bind to variables instead of hardcoded values.

1. **Extract tokens** — run storysync to detect and extract tokens from the project:

```bash
npx storysync tokens --json --project .
```

This auto-detects the token source (a Tailwind config or Tailwind v4 `@theme` block, CSS custom properties, or theme files) and outputs structured JSON with collections of tokens by category (colors, spacing, typography, radius, shadows). If a `warnings` entry says storysync can't read or find part of the Tailwind config, or doesn't follow it, such as a preset from a package, a config built by a function, or a file it names, or that it read no theme from the config, tell the user what it names: those tokens are missing from the output, so they won't reach Figma. If one says it read another source rather than a `tailwind.config.mts` or `.cts`, tell the user, who can pass `--source tailwind` to read the config. If it returns no collections, skip to Components.

2. **Preview tokens** — optionally, run `npx storysync tokens --project .` (without `--json`) for a human-readable preview, or add `--all` to show every token.

3. **Create Figma variable collections** — use the JSON output to create Figma variables via `use_figma` (with the file key and `skillNames: "figma-use"`), one collection at a time. Reuse a collection of the same name rather than creating a second. A call takes at most 50,000 characters of code, and one that runs too long fails with `Script exceeded time limit`; creating variables is slow, so split a large collection (a full color palette runs to hundreds) across calls of a few dozen variables each. Use Figma `COLOR` type for colors and `FLOAT` type for spacing, radius, and font sizes. A Tailwind v4 `@theme` names its typography tokens by namespace: `text/*` are font sizes, `font/weight/*` weights, `leading/*` line heights and `tracking/*` letter spacing, all `FLOAT`, and the other `font/*` are font families, `STRING`. Set each `FLOAT` variable from its token's `px`, not its `value`: `px` is the value in pixels with rem (16px) and `calc()` worked out, so `calc(0.625rem - 4px)` is 6. A weight or a unitless line height has no `px`, and its `value` is the number to set. Leave out, and say which, any other `FLOAT` token with no `px` (one in em or %, as letter spacing often is), and any token a `tokens` warning starting `Unresolved:` names, whose `var()` is set nowhere storysync reads (next/font, say, sets a font's at runtime). The tokens a warning starting `Skipped dynamic value:` names, a value the config only computes when it runs, such as a template literal that interpolates or a `theme()` call, are out of the output already: name them too. The tokens such a warning says were not read from `@theme`, shadcn/ui's `font/sans` and `font/mono` among them, are out of the output already: name them all the same. A `value` can keep a `var()` that did resolve, as `calc(var(--radius) - 4px)` in `:root` does; set that token from its `px` or `hex` as usual. If no token in the output has a `px` at all, an older storysync ran: use a plain rem `value` times 16, or a px one as is, in place of `px`. Set each color from its token's `hex`, not its `value`: `figma.util.rgba(token.hex ?? token.value)`. `figma.util.rgb` and `rgba` take only hex, `rgb()`, `hsl()` and `lab()`, so a `value` in `oklch()` or another CSS Color 4 space, or bare HSL channels such as `0 0% 100%`, throws. A color token with no `hex` is one storysync couldn't convert, such as `currentColor` or an unresolved `var()`; if `rgba` throws on its `value` too, leave it out and say which.

4. **Verify** — confirm the variable collections were created with the expected count, less the tokens you left out. If any are missing, retry.

## Components

1. **Count the components, and agree on what to push.** List them first; this reads only Storybook's list of components, so it takes seconds:

```bash
npx storysync list --storybook http://localhost:6006 --json
```

This outputs each component's Storybook `id`, `name`, and a `title`/`category` reflecting its place in Storybook's sidebar (e.g. `id: "forms-button"`, `title: "Forms/Button"`, `category: "Forms"`). Use the category and title to organize the Figma file — see step 4 below. No other component shares an `id`, where a `name` can be shared: `--components` and `inspect` take a title or an ID as well as a name.

   Step 3 reads each component's docs and renders every variant in a browser, several seconds a component and more on a busy Storybook (134 components took about 17 minutes). **If there are more than about 20 components, stop and ask the user what to push before measuring anything:** all of them, some categories (name each with how many components it has), or some components. Pass what they choose to step 3 as `--components`, by ID. With 20 or fewer, push them all. If a command fails because Storybook didn't answer in time, a busy or shared Storybook is slow rather than down: run it again with the flag its error names raised, `--request-timeout 900000` for a request, which has five minutes by default, or `--connect-timeout 180000` for connecting.

2. **Inspect individual components** — for detailed prop-to-variant mapping:

```bash
npx storysync inspect --storybook http://localhost:6006 --component Button
```

   `npx storysync map --storybook http://localhost:6006 --json` maps every component's variant properties without rendering anything. The push doesn't need it: snap's output carries the same `id`, `title`, `category` and `variantProperties`, and a map first would read every component's docs twice.

3. **REQUIRED — Measure the styling, don't infer it.** Do not proceed to step 5 without a snap taken in this session: every Figma layer the push builds comes from it.

```bash
mkdir -p .storysync
STORYSYNC_PROGRESS=1 npx storysync snap --storybook http://localhost:6006 --variants all --json > .storysync/snap-out.json 2> .storysync/snap-progress.log; echo "exit $?" >> .storysync/snap-progress.log
```

   Add `--components` with the IDs step 1 agreed on, unless the user chose every component. This renders each variant in a real browser and reports what the browser actually computed, so the values are measured rather than guessed: the variant's outer element and every element inside it that draws, with its text, its icons' shapes and its pictures. This measures **every combination** of the component's variant props, and step 5 builds one Figma variant per snap variant, whatever its status: a Figma component set needs every combination to exist, or the variant picker has nothing to switch to. **If a component carries a `cap` field** (its combinations exceed the limit, 256 by default), snap measured only a subset and warned about it; the cap's `uncovered` list names any values that subset left out entirely. **Stop and ask the user before building that component.** Tell them its total and the limit, and offer: build the measured subset; measure it again with `--components <its ID> --merge --max-combinations <total>` to measure all of them (very large component sets get slow in Figma); or narrow which props are variants: re-run snap with `--exclude-props` naming the props that don't change how it looks, such as `modal` or `loop`, which leaves them out of every component. Do not offer to fill the unmeasured combinations in from source: they can be measured by raising the limit, and a value the tool can read must never be guessed. Do not choose for them. Run it *now*, in this session — do not reuse a `styles.json` from an earlier run, which may describe code that has since changed.

   snap measures each component from a story it picks. If the user wants a component measured from another of its stories, one that shows it with an icon, say, add `--stories <its ID>=<story ID>`, as in `--stories ui-base-button=ui-base-button--with-icon`, taking the story's ID from the component's `storyIds` in step 1's list; separate several pairs with commas. snap refuses a story that isn't the component's, before measuring anything.

   **Run it in the background, and check how it ended before building.** A shell command can be stopped after a few minutes, and snap stopped part-way writes nothing. So run the command above in the background (with the terminal tool's background option where it has one, else wrapped in `( ... ) &`), as any storysync command that may take more than a minute or two. `STORYSYNC_PROGRESS=1` makes it log a line per component and variant, such as `snap 12/134 UI Base/Command, variant 3/32`: check on it with `tail -n 1 .storysync/snap-progress.log` until the last line is its `exit` code. `exit 0`: it finished, and wrote `.storysync/snaps/styles.json` and `meta.json`. `exit 1`: it failed, with the reason as `{"error": ...}` in `.storysync/snap-out.json` (Storybook unreachable or too slow, so raise the flag its error names; a `--components` name that matches nothing; a `--merge` it refused, below; or the browser closing twice); an option it can't take, such as a `--timeout` that isn't a number, is said in the progress log instead. `exit 130`, `143` or `129`: it was stopped, by Ctrl-C, SIGTERM (a command timeout, say) or SIGHUP, and the log says so. After any exit but 0, the `styles.json` and `meta.json` in `.storysync/snaps` are an earlier run's: never build from them; deal with what it said, and run it again. After `exit 0`, check too that `meta.json` is this run's: its `measuredAt` is after you started it, or, after a `--merge`, which keeps `measuredAt` at the oldest component's time on purpose, its `componentsMeasuredAt` time for each component you named is. Read what it recorded from `.storysync/snaps/styles.json` on disk, a component at a time (with `jq` or `node`, say): `snap-out.json` holds the same JSON on one line, which tools cut short.

   snap writes each component's styles as earlier versions did, full styles for a base variant and only what each other variant changes, and its **element tree**: `tree.base` lists every element of the base variant that draws, each keyed by its place in the component (`card-header/card-title`, `#text`), with its box, its styles and its text; each other variant's `treeDelta` says what differs from it; and `tree.assets` holds the component's icons as SVG and its pictures, whose thumbnails snap saves under `.storysync/snaps/assets/`. **You don't translate any of it into Figma yourself:** step 5's `figma code` writes every call from it, and step 6's `verify` scores what Figma holds element by element. What you read in the file is what needs you or the user: each component's `warnings`, `cap` and `argsNotInDocs`, and each variant's `status`, `appliedVia` and `argsChecked`, below.

   **Variants whose `status` is not `"ok"` were not measured.** A `timeout` loading the story was already tried twice; a busy Storybook times out stories that render, so first measure those components again, in the background as above, adding `--components <their IDs> --merge` and a longer `--timeout`, such as 30000, to the same command. `--merge` measures only those and merges them into `.storysync/snaps/styles.json`, keeping every other component as it was measured, and gives `meta.json` each component's own time, in `componentsMeasuredAt`, with `measuredAt` the oldest of them, so `verify --strict-age` judges the snap by its oldest part; it refuses a snap it can't add to, such as one taken with another `--storybook`, `--variants` or `--exclude-props`. Never re-measure some components with `--components` alone: that writes a `styles.json` of those alone, and step 6's `verify` would score only them. An `arg_rejected` variant is one the story wouldn't render with the value asked for, because its argTypes don't allow it, and that snap couldn't set through Storybook's preview either: snap's warning names the story change that lets it, such as adding the value to the arg's `options`. Name it in the summary with that change, and once the story allows it, measure those components again with `--components <their IDs> --merge`. What still fails, step 5 builds as a placeholder, a dashed frame labeled `Not measured: <status>`, which `verify` lists as not measured and never scores, and `--strict-measured` fails on. Never style one from the component's source: a variant styled from source would be scored against a measurement it doesn't have. Name each in the summary with the story change it needs.

   **Keep every snap of a push alike.** An audit must tell which props you left out with `--exclude-props`: pass the same list to every `snap` you run for this push, re-runs included, and name those props in the summary. snap records them as `excludeProps` in `styles.json`, where an audit reads them, and a run without the flag measures them again and drops that record (a `--merge` without it refuses to run). Pass the same `--stories` to each run too: a run that measures some components again leaves out the pairs for the others, and `--merge` measures a component again from the story it was measured from where `--stories` names none.

   **Checkpoint before step 5**: every variant was measured, or you know why it wasn't and the user will hear it in the summary. If all of a component's variants measured identically, `snap` says why it thinks so, by what each variant's markup did. Where they also `rendered the same markup`, nothing the args set reached the page: the story may not pass its args through. Still build what was measured, which is what the story actually renders, and say so in the summary; the fix belongs in the story. Where `their markup changed with the args`, the story passes them through, and the props the warning names change nothing snap measures, likely only behavior, as input-otp's `textAlign` and `pushPasswordManagerStrategy` do: never say the story drops its args. Ask the user, in the same message as any cap or trigger question below, whether to leave those props out, re-running snap with the warning's `--exclude-props` added to any this push already passes, or to build the identical variants as measured; name the props in the summary either way. Where the warning says both, the markup couldn't settle it: say both in the summary. In every case, do not substitute values from source: a variant styled from source would be scored against a measurement it does not match. The same goes for a warning that a prop's values all measured the same: build what was measured, and name it in the summary with the fix the warning gives. A warning that the story's URL wouldn't take some values, which snap then set through Storybook's preview, means those variants were measured as the component renders them (their `appliedVia` is `"preview"`, and one whose `status` isn't `"ok"` failed after that, like any other variant not measured): build them like the rest, and name the story change the warning gives in the summary. A variant snap marked `argsChecked: false` was measured, but snap couldn't read Storybook's preview to check that the story was given its values, so it may be the story's default under the variant's name: measure those components again with `--components <their IDs> --merge`, and if snap still can't check them, build what was measured, and name them in the summary. A component's `argsNotInDocs` lists choices its story declares that Storybook's docs don't list among its props, such as a Sheet's `side` or a Button's `disabled`: they aren't variant properties, so the Figma set won't have them. Name them in the summary for each component, and say that they become variants once the component's own prop types declare them, which is where Storybook's docs read props from.

   **Stop and ask about every component snap measured as something else.** When snap warns that it measured a closed trigger (a dialog, popover, sheet, menu or tooltip whose content renders only while open) or a layout wrapper rather than the component, the Figma component would be that trigger or wrapper, and `verify` would score it as faithful. Before building any of them, ask the user once, listing each such component with the element snap measured, whether to: **build what was measured**, and say so in the summary and in Figma, on a line of each such set's description that starts `storysync:`, which step 5's label call sets; **skip them**, naming them in the summary as not pushed, and giving their IDs to step 5's `figma code` and step 6's `verify` as `--skip`, which leaves them out of the push and out of the score, and names them under it, since they stay in `styles.json` and `verify` would otherwise list each of their variants as missing from Figma, which is no reason to build them; or **stop**, so they can give each a story that renders it open (with `defaultOpen`, say) or on its own, as the warning says, then measure those again with `--components <their IDs> --merge`. Ask it in the same message as any cap question, and do not choose for them. Never style such a component from source instead: it would be scored against a measurement it does not match.

4. **The Figma file is organized by Storybook hierarchy.** Step 5's calls make a Figma page per `category`, the whole of it (e.g. `Forms`, `Data Display`, `ui/base`), and put a component without a category on a `Components` page, which mirrors the Storybook sidebar so designers can find things where they expect them. Each component's set is named by the last segment of its `title`, the name Storybook's sidebar shows (`Button` for `Forms/Button`, and `Tooltip` for `ui/base/Tooltip` though its `name` is `TooltipContent`), or by its `name` when it has no title, so components that share a name stay apart: `ui/base/Button` and `ui/radix/Button` go to the pages `ui/base` and `ui/radix`. A category's page is found ignoring case, spaces, `/`, `-` and `_`, so a page an earlier push named `UI Base` is still the page for `ui/base`. A set is found on its page by its name, inside a section or frame a designer moved it into too, and where none has the name, by the Storybook `id` an earlier push stored on it, so a set a designer renamed is still updated in place, under its own name; one an earlier push named by a component's `name`, where that differs, is found the same way: name it in the summary. You make no page or set yourself.

5. **Build with the calls `figma code` writes, and write no styling code.** Every `use_figma` call of the push is a file `figma code` generates from the snap. You send each one unchanged and save Figma's response to it unchanged; storysync does the rest. Never write, edit, shorten or summarize a call's code, and never edit a saved response: a call changed on the way builds what snap didn't measure, and `verify` checks each response's checksum.

   First save the tokens, once Tokens step 3 has made their variables, so the build binds each fill, stroke, text color and corner radius to its variable (Variable binding, below):

```bash
npx storysync tokens --json --project . > .storysync/tokens.json
```

   Then write the calls, with `--components` and the IDs step 1 agreed on, unless the user chose every component, and `--skip` with any the user chose not to push in step 3:

```bash
npx storysync figma code --components <the agreed IDs>
```

   It reads `.storysync/snaps/styles.json` and `.storysync/tokens.json`, and writes `.storysync/figma/plan.json` and the calls under `.storysync/figma/calls/`, in this order: the builds, a page at a time, several sets a call; an `images` call for each page that shows a picture; one `label` call; and the reads, a page at a time. It prints each call, then its warnings. Note each warning for the summary:

   - `built as a "Not measured" placeholder`: variants snap couldn't measure (step 3), each built as a dashed frame labeled `Not measured: <status>`.
   - `left unbound: it matches …, and no class names one`: a value several variables share, left unbound rather than bound to a guess (Variable binding, below).
   - `… is not built`: something Figma can't draw, such as a radial gradient or a backdrop filter. `verify` lists it under coverage.
   - `… so it is built without auto layout`: a frame whose children Figma's auto layout can't place where the browser did, such as a wrap onto two lines, built with each child at its measured place.
   - `not built: its N nodes need N characters of code`: a variant too big for one call, which `verify` reports missing from Figma.
   - `holds one variant of N characters, over --max-chars` or `of N nodes, over --max-nodes`: a variant too big for the calls' usual size, in a call of its own, which is still within `use_figma`'s 50,000 characters. If it throws `Script exceeded time limit`, say so in the summary.
   - `no tokens at …`: the tokens weren't saved, so nothing would be bound: save them as above and run `figma code` again.

   Running `figma code` again writes the plan anew, and removes the calls and results an earlier run left in `.storysync/figma`.

   **Send the calls in `seq` order.** Read `.storysync/figma/plan.json`. For each entry of its `calls`, in order, call `use_figma` with `code` the contents of the entry's `file` (under `.storysync/figma/`), exactly as they are, `description` the entry's `description`, your `fileKey`, and `skillNames: "figma-use"`. A file is at most 45,000 characters, or up to `use_figma`'s 50,000 for a call that holds one large variant, which `figma code` warns about, and its first line names its call. Then **save the response unchanged** to the entry's `result` path, under `.storysync/figma/` (`results/01.json`): the text `use_figma` returned, whole, as it came, before you send the next call. Don't reformat it, trim it or copy parts of it: `verify` reads the files itself.

   A build call restyles a set an earlier push built in place: it finds each variant by name and each layer by the key it stored on it, restyles what changed, adds what's missing and removes the layers snap no longer measures, so node ids, and the instances built on them, survive. An icon whose shapes were recolored or changed in Figma is imported again, as measured, under a new node id. Where an earlier storysync built the set root-only, the build replaces that push's label and icon layers, and counts them in its response's `migrated`. A layer a designer added inside a variant is left alone and counted in `designerLayers`. Each set is laid out as a grid in snap's order, the first property's values as rows, with a variant snap doesn't have in a row below the grid (`extra`), and the variant of each property's first value top left, where Figma takes its default from: the component's own default, unless a property is marked `defaultAssumed: true`, whose default Storybook's docs don't give, so its `defaultValue` is a guess: name those in the summary. A new set goes below everything on its page. A build's response gives, per set, `added`, `updated`, `migrated`, `designerLayers`, `extra`, `overlaps` and `absoluteContainers`, and for the call `fonts`, the stand-ins for fonts Figma lacks, `unbound` and `fallbacks`: keep them for the summary.

   **Pictures.** A build call whose response lists `uploads`, `[[nodeId, assetId], …]`, has left those layers empty for their pictures: `use_figma` can't create an image, and `upload_assets` is how one gets into Figma. Right after that call:

   1. Call `upload_assets` with your `fileKey`, `count` the number of uploads, `nodeIds` the uploads' node ids in the order listed, and `scaleMode: "FILL"`, leaving out `batchCommit`.
   2. Save its response unchanged beside the call's result, with `.upload.json` for `.json` (`results/03.upload.json` for call 3).
   3. Run `npx storysync figma upload --plan .storysync/figma/plan.json --seq 3`, with the call's `seq`. It posts each thumbnail snap saved to the URL `upload_assets` gave its layer, as its own type, and prints a line for each.

   Each URL takes one upload: if `figma upload` fails, call `upload_assets` again for new URLs, save that response over the last, and run it again. The plan's `images` call fills the other layers that show the same picture, and a later push reuses a picture already in the file rather than uploading it again. An `images` response that lists asset ids in `unfilled` found no uploaded picture for them: check that `figma upload` posted every upload of the build calls that listed them, run it again with new `upload_assets` URLs where it failed, then send the `images` call again.

   **Reads that continue.** A read call stops before its response nears `use_figma`'s 20kb and says in `more` where it stopped. After saving a read call's response, run `npx storysync figma next --plan .storysync/figma/plan.json --seq <its seq>`. It says there is nothing left to continue, or prints the file of a call that reads the rest, its description, and where to save its response (`results/07b.json`). Send that file the same way, save its response there, and run `figma next` again, until it says there is nothing left. Do the same after sending a read call again: the continuations saved after it the time before hold Figma as it was then, so `figma next` asks for each of them again, saying it `was read before the result it continues`; send it and save its response over the old one.

   **If a call throws**, Figma's error is its response, and a build call removes the variants it created for that set before it fails, so none is left loose on the page. Deal with what the error says, then send the same call again, saving its response over the error. Every call can be sent again: a build finds what it made before and changes only what differs.

   - `Script exceeded time limit`: the call did more than Figma allows a call. Run `figma code` again with `--max-nodes 200`, which makes smaller calls, and send the new plan's calls from the first: those already done change nothing the second time.
   - `The page has COMPONENT_SET "…" (…), COMPONENT_SET "…" (…): ask the user which to update`: two sets of the component's name, or with its id, are on the page. Ask the user which is the component's, and in a call of its own, rename the other or clear its id (`node.setSharedPluginData('storysync', 'id', '')`).
   - `… it is another component: ask the user whether it is …`: a set of this name carries another component's id. If the user says it is this component's, clear its id in a call of its own; otherwise skip the component: run `figma code` again with it in `--skip`, and send the new plan.
   - `… this part builds a COMPONENT: ask the user whether to convert it or skip it`, or `… this part builds a COMPONENT_SET …`: a component with no variant properties is one plain component, not a set, and a push before 0.4.0 built one as a set of one variant named `''`, which Figma renames `=` and marks as broken; a plain component an earlier push built may since have gained variant properties. Ask the user whether to convert it or skip the component, and do neither on your own. To convert the old set, in a call of its own, put its one variant where the set is, in the set's parent, and give it the set's name, which keeps every instance of it linked (`const { parent, x, y, name } = set; parent.insertChild(parent.children.indexOf(set), variant); Object.assign(variant, { x, y, name })`), then remove the set if Figma left it behind (`if (!set.removed) set.remove()`); to convert a plain component, combine it into a set of its own where it is (`const { parent, x, y, name } = component; const set = figma.combineAsVariants([component], parent); Object.assign(set, { x, y, name })`), and name the variant from snap's default combination. Then send the call again.
   - `The set "…" has auto layout (…)`: auto layout would place the variants itself. Ask the user whether to turn it off (`componentSet.layoutMode = 'NONE'`, in a call of its own) and send the call again, or to skip the set, saying in the summary that it was not pushed. Do neither on your own.
   - `The set "…" has 2 variants named "…", not one`: an earlier push or a designer made the variant twice. Report it. Remove the stale copy only after checking which one is current, or after asking the user which to keep, then send the call again.
   - `Variants "…" and "…" overlap`, `… is not where the layout put it`, `… lies outside the set`, or `The set's layers are not in the order it was laid out in`: Figma didn't lay the set out as the build asked. Report it with the set's name; don't move variants by hand.
   - `Found 0 sets with storysync id …` from a read call, or `No page named "…"` or `No page "…"`: the set's build call didn't run, or failed: send it, then this call. The label call lists such sets in its response's `missing` instead. `Found 2 sets with storysync id …`: a copy of the set carries its id; ask the user which is the component's, and clear the other's id in a call of its own.
   - `Variants … and … of "…" carry the slug …: one was duplicated`: a designer duplicated a variant. Report it, and ask the user which to keep.

   A response's `overlaps` other than 0 means its set lies on other sets, as a push before 0.4.0 left every set at `0,0`. Ask the user whether to move it below the rest of its page, in a call of its own (`node.y = Math.max(...node.parent.children.filter((n) => n !== node).map((n) => n.y + n.height)) + 100; node.x = 0`), and say in the summary which sets overlap and whether they were moved; never move one on your own. The `label` call writes the line of each set's description that starts `storysync:`, for a component snap measured from a closed trigger or a layout wrapper, keeping every other line, and removes Figma's default page `Page 1` while it is empty and not the file's only page: its response's `pageOne` says which (`removed`, or why it was kept).

6. **Score what Figma holds.** Once every call is sent and every response saved:

```bash
npx storysync verify --plan .storysync/figma/plan.json --strict-age
```

   `verify` reads the responses saved for the read calls, assembles them into `.storysync/figma-readback.json`, which can be committed for CI, and scores Figma against snap element by element: each element's place and size, fills, strokes, radii, shadows, opacity and blend, a text's characters, font and fit, an icon's shapes and colors, and a picture's bytes. Never write `.storysync/figma-readback.json` or a saved response yourself. Add `--skip` with the IDs of any component the user chose not to push (step 3), or that you skipped in step 5, as in `--skip ui-base-dialog,ui-base-sheet`: `verify` leaves them out of the score and names them under it, where otherwise it lists each of their variants as missing from Figma.

   It starts with the score:

```text
Fidelity: 99.3% of elements (287 of 289 verified), 99.6% of properties (3,412/3,426)
  worst: ui/base/Attachment 96.7% of elements
  missing 1 · drifted 1 · extra 0 · designer layers 0 · not measured 14
  coverage 99.8%: not scored: per-side border colors on ui/base/InputOTP › input-otp-slot:6
  ~ ui/base/Switch size=sm › switch-thumb: fills measured #ffffff, Figma #f5f5f5 (node 14:9)
```

   **Element fidelity is the score**: each element scores the share of its properties Figma matches, and 0 when it is missing, of another kind (a frame where snap measured an icon), hidden, or has the wrong characters, shapes or picture. The share of properties is beside it. The lines under it name what differs, missing elements first, each by component, variant, key and Figma node id. The coverage line names what Figma can't represent, which is listed rather than scored.

   **Do not fix drift by hand.** The build comes from the measurement, so a layer edited in Figma is one the next push undoes, and what made it differ comes back. If `verify` reports drift, send that component's build calls again once (the calls in plan.json whose `components` include it), then its read calls, continuing them with `figma next` as before, and run `verify` again. Report what remains, by key and node id: as a font stand-in, a text a few percent wider or narrower where the build responses' `fonts` name one, or as a limit of the mapping. **Stop after two rounds** and report the residual score. `use_figma` calls are rate-limited and becoming a paid feature; an unbounded repair loop burns that budget for diminishing returns.

   What else it can say, and what to do:

   - `planned read call N wasn't read whole`: the plan's lines below the score say why. `call N: no result saved at …`: send that read call, or the continuation `figma next` asks for, and save its response. `… holds no read result`: the call failed, and its response is the error; deal with it as step 5 says. `… was read before …, which it continues`: a read call was sent again without its continuations; run `figma next` for it, as above, and send what it asks for. Then run `verify` again. The variants the call didn't read are counted as `not read`, never as missing from Figma, and aren't scored, and the run fails `--strict` until they are read.
   - A readback entry that `does not match its checksum`: a saved response was changed after Figma returned it. Send that read call again, save its response unchanged, and run `verify` again. Never edit a saved response to make it pass. An entry that `is incomplete`, `is stale`, read before the snap, or is a part that doesn't add up, is read again the same way, now.
   - An entry sealed under another name than the variant snap measured: a variant renamed in Figma. Report it.
   - `read back without its tree, so only its root was compared`: those entries were read with the root-only readback. Send this plan's read calls.
   - `a layer nothing keyed, "…" (TYPE, node …): a designer's, not scored`: a layer a designer added inside a variant, which the build leaves alone. It fails `--strict`. Ask the user whether it belongs there; if it does, run `verify` again with `--allow-designer-layers`, and name it in the summary either way.
   - `but snap no longer measures it`: a layer an earlier push built for an element the code no longer renders. Sending the component's build call removes it.
   - `not measured, so Figma holds a placeholder for it`: a variant snap couldn't measure (step 3). Not scored, and `--strict-measured` fails on it.
   - `made this plan from it`: the snap was taken again after the plan was written. Run `figma code` again and send its calls.
   - What snap warned about the measurement, under the score (a closed trigger, variants or values that measured alike, values Storybook wouldn't take or took only through its preview, args snap couldn't check, a font the browser couldn't render, and any other warning, which it quotes as snap wrote it): report it, as step 3 says. With `--json`, these are its `caveats`.
   - `! snap recorded a failure`: the measurement itself is incomplete, not Figma. Where it names a component's error, measure that component again (step 3, with `--merge`); where it says components share a key, or that the snap recorded no components, do what snap warned and run the whole snap again.
   - `Variables:` the element-tree readback doesn't read variable bindings yet, so a token detached in Figma at the same value can't be seen. Say so in the summary.

7. Summarize what was synced: token collections created, and that they hold the light theme alone where a `tokens` warning starts `Only the light theme is read`, the tokens left out (Tokens step 3), components grouped by page, whether the label call removed Figma's default page `Page 1` or why it kept it (its `pageOne`), variant counts, the components built as one plain component for having no variant properties, **the fidelity score, worded as `verify` words it: element fidelity, with the share of properties beside it**, and where its line says entries were `unverified, not scored`, how many entries it left unverified, which the score doesn't cover; the coverage `verify` gives, with what it couldn't score; the variants built as a `Not measured` placeholder, each with the story change it needs; any components whose stories did not pass their args through, any props snap found reach a component but change nothing it measures, and what the user chose for them, any variant snap marked `argsChecked: false`, each component's `argsNotInDocs`, each component measured from the story `--stories` named, with the story, for an audit to measure from it too, each component snap measured as a closed trigger or a layout wrapper and what the user chose for it (step 3), what `verify` printed under the score about what snap measured; the frames built without auto layout (`figma code`'s warnings and the build responses' `absoluteContainers`), the layers of an earlier root-only push the build replaced (`migrated`), and the designer layers `verify` listed, with what the user chose for them; the values left unbound because several variables share them, from `figma code`'s warnings and the build responses' `unbound`; every font stand-in the build responses returned in `fonts` (a CSS keyword such as `system-ui` set in Inter, a platform face such as Menlo in Roboto Mono), which snap measured that text in too where its `fontStandIns` says so, and any `fallbacks` they list, where `kept on one line` or `cut short` means Figma sets a text wider than snap measured it, which a snap taken again measures in Figma's font; which variant Figma will treat as a set's default where that is not the component's default (step 5); any set skipped for its auto layout, any component skipped for a set or plain component of its name an earlier push left (step 5), any set or component a build returned `overlaps` for, and whether the user had it moved; any picture `figma upload` couldn't post, or an `images` response left `unfilled`; that `verify` can't yet see variable bindings in an element-tree readback; and any failures or caps.

## Variable binding

`figma code` binds each fill, stroke, text color, icon color and corner radius to a variable from `.storysync/tokens.json`, the `tokens --json` output Components step 5 saves, so that when tokens change in code and storysync runs again, updating the variables updates every component. A measured value matches a token when it equals the token's `hex` for a color, or its `px` for a radius. A translucent color, `#rrggbbaa`, matches a token of its `#rrggbb`, and is bound with the paint's opacity at its alpha, as Tailwind's `border-destructive/30` is the `destructive` token at 30%. Several tokens often share a value: on shadcn/ui, `#171717` is five color tokens, `primary` and `sidebar/primary` among them. Then the classes snap recorded on the element pick the one the source names: `bg-primary` binds the fill to `primary`, `text-card-foreground` the text to `card/foreground`, `border-input` the stroke to `input`, and `rounded-lg` the radius to `lg`. Where no class names one, the value is left unbound, and `figma code` warns with the variables it matches: list them in the summary rather than bind one by hand. The build finds each variable by its name in the file, as Tokens step 3 created it from `tokens`, so a name Figma has none of, or two of, leaves the value unbound too, listed in the build response's `unbound`. Shadows stay unbound, and a project that uses Tailwind's default spacing, type or shadow scales has no tokens for them, so those values stay as measured.

## Root-only snaps (`--root-only`)

storysync 0.4.0 measured each variant's outer element alone, its fill, border, radius, padding, gap and one text style, and the agent wrote the plugin code that built it, as the steps below describe. That path remains for a push the user asks to make that way: to restyle the sets a 0.4.0 push built without adding their inner layers, say, or to compare with 0.4.0's numbers. It compares only the outer element, so a set pushed this way can score 100% with its icon missing or its title in the wrong place, and `verify` says of each component that only its outer element was compared. Take the element-tree path above unless the user asks for this one. `figma code` refuses a snap taken this way, and points here.

For a root-only push, Components steps 1 and 2 are as above, step 3's snap adds `--root-only`, and steps 3 to 7 below take the place of the rest:

```bash
mkdir -p .storysync
STORYSYNC_PROGRESS=1 npx storysync snap --storybook http://localhost:6006 --variants all --root-only --json > .storysync/snap-out.json 2> .storysync/snap-progress.log; echo "exit $?" >> .storysync/snap-progress.log
```

Everything step 3 says above about running it, its exit codes, caps, statuses and warnings holds. A root-only snap holds no element trees, and `snap --merge` refuses to mix one with a snap of trees.

3. **Read the measurements**, from `.storysync/snaps/styles.json` on disk, a component at a time. Do not proceed to step 5 without concrete values: a `use_figma` call containing only variant names produces a useless component library.

   Output is a base variant plus per-variant deltas; a variant's full styles are the base with its `delta` applied. `variantProperties` lists the component's variant properties in order, each with its values and its default: step 5 lays the set out by it. Translate directly: `backgroundColor` → fill (`null` means no fill, not black), `backgroundClip` → where that fill stops (absent, CSS's `border-box`, it runs under a transparent or translucent border, and `padding-box` stops it at the border: see below; build `content-box` as `padding-box` and name it in the summary, and give the text the fill, not the frame, for `text`, as Tailwind's `bg-clip-text` gradient labels have), `color`/`text.color` → text fill, `padding` → auto-layout padding, `gap.column`, or `gap.row` when `flexDirection` is `column` or `column-reverse`, → `itemSpacing`, the gap along the layout's direction (`verify` compares a flex element's gap on that axis alone), `borderRadiusUniform` (else `borderRadius`) → `cornerRadius`, `borderUniform` → stroke (`null` means none; a `color` of `null` is a transparent border, a stroke with an invisible paint: see below), with its `style` as the stroke's `dashPattern`, for a width `w`: none for `solid`, `[3w, 3w]` for `dashed`, `[w, w]` for `dotted` (Figma's strokes have no `double`, `groove`, `ridge`, `inset` or `outset`: build those solid, and name them in the summary, since `verify` reports their style as drift no edit can fix), `boxShadow[]` → one effect per layer, `INNER_SHADOW` where `inset` is true, a ring like `ring-inset` included, else `DROP_SHADOW`, with `offset` from `offsetX`/`offsetY`, `radius` from `blur`, and `spread` (`verify` doesn't read effects back, so it won't flag a shadow built wrong), `display: flex` + `flexDirection` → auto-layout direction, `fontSize`/`fontWeight`/`fontFamily` → text style (fonts below), `text.layout` → how the label fits its box: absent, the text hugs it (`textAutoResize = 'WIDTH_AND_HEIGHT'`); present, a fixed width of `layout.width` with `textAutoResize = 'HEIGHT'`, so it wraps where the browser did, and with a `truncation` also `textTruncation = 'ENDING'` and `maxLines = layout.lines`, so it ends in an ellipsis after as many lines (Figma has no flat cut, so build a `clip` the same way and name it in the summary; `verify` scores a label that hugs where the browser cut it off as drift, since it runs past its frame), a `width` or `height` of 0 → 0.01, the least Figma's `resize` takes (a closed collapsible's content renders nothing, and is built as next to nothing). A color written `#rrggbbaa` is translucent: give it as `#rrggbb` with the paint's `opacity` at `aa` / 255, so `#4b556322` is `#4b5563` at an opacity of 34 / 255, about 0.133, or for a shadow as the effect color's `a`.

   Five things that go wrong by default, in ways `verify` either won't catch or won't explain:

   - **Stroke alignment: the stroke adds to the size.** snap's `width`/`height` are the element's outer size, border included, and the Figma frame's outer size, its own size plus the stroke outside it, must match them. On an element sized by its content — every inline or inline-flex component, and any auto-layout frame you let hug — a CSS border always adds to the outer size, whatever `boxSizing` says; `box-sizing` only changes how an *explicit* width or height is read. Figma's default, `INSIDE` on a hugging frame, eats into the padding instead and leaves the variant short by twice the border width. So build a border one of two ways on a hugging frame: `strokeAlign = 'OUTSIDE'`, or `INSIDE` with `strokesIncludedInLayout = true`, which adds the stroke to the frame's own size; and on a frame you give a fixed size, `INSIDE` at the measured `width`/`height`. **A transparent border still takes its space.** A `borderUniform` whose `color` is `null`, such as CSS `border: 1px solid transparent`, is in snap's `width`/`height` like any other, so give it a stroke of that weight, aligned the same way, with a paint that draws nothing (`{ type: 'SOLID', color: { r: 0, g: 0, b: 0 }, opacity: 0 }`), never no stroke: without one the variant is short by twice the border. Step 6 reads the size from the geometry, which counts a stroke whether its paint shows or not, and reads that stroke's color back as `null`, as snap measured it. **Where the fill stops is `backgroundClip`**, which picks between the two for a transparent or translucent border. The browser paints the background under such a border unless it is clipped to the padding box. With `backgroundClip: padding-box`, as shadcn/ui's Button has (`bg-clip-padding`), the fill stops at the border, as Figma's does at an `OUTSIDE` stroke, so build that border `OUTSIDE` (on a fixed-size frame, at the measured size less twice the border's weight). Without it, CSS's `border-box`, as on shadcn/ui's Badge, the fill runs under the border, as Figma's does under an `INSIDE` stroke, so build it `INSIDE`, with `strokesIncludedInLayout = true` on a hugging frame. Either way the size and the stroke's color read back as measured; never paint the stroke to look filled, which `verify` scores as a border color the code does not have.
   - **Lay the variants out.** A component set is a frame containing its variants, each needing its own x/y, and the frame must be grown to fit. Created without positions they all land at `0,0`, stacked and clipped by a frame still sized for one. Position each variant in a grid — one row per value of the first variant property, the remaining combinations across — with spacing, and size the set to contain them. With every combination built, a single row of dozens of variants is unreadable. **Order the grid by snap's `variantProperties`, never by the set's children**: their order is whatever an earlier push or a designer left, and a set laid out in it gets rows whose columns disagree. Step 5 says how.
   - **Assert the layout before returning.** After positioning, check that no two variants' bounding boxes intersect and that the set's bounds contain every child. Geometry comparison catches variants clipped by an undersized frame, but two same-sized variants stacked inside an adequately sized one measure correctly and stay invisible — so the check has to happen here, at write time.
   - **Figma's plugin context has only Google Fonts.** `listAvailableFontsAsync` returns Google families and nothing else — no Arial, no Helvetica, no SF Pro, no Segoe UI, no Menlo. A designer sees those in the desktop app's picker because it reads their *local* system fonts, but the environment `use_figma` runs in does not have them, and `figma.loadFontAsync` throws on one. Never let a font fail the push: load each text layer's font in a `try`, the measured family first, then a stand-in at the measured weight, Roboto Mono for a monospace face (Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, `ui-monospace`, `monospace`), Noto Serif for a serif one (Georgia, `ui-serif`, `serif`) and Inter for any other, trying each style in both of Figma's spellings (`Semi Bold`, `SemiBold`), and a weight the family lacks as the next one toward `Regular` (Roboto Mono stops at Bold, so 800 loads as Bold, which `verify` scores as `fontWeight` drift). Have the part return each stand-in it used as `fonts`, the family or weight it loaded where either differs from the measured one, not a mere difference of spelling, such as `{ "system-ui 600": "Inter Semi Bold", "Menlo 400": "Roboto Mono Regular" }`. Name every one in the summary. A CSS keyword, such as `system-ui`, `sans-serif` or `ui-sans-serif`, is the platform's own font, which no Figma font is named after (Tailwind's default `-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, ...` measures as `system-ui`), and `verify` doesn't score it, so its stand-in isn't drift. A platform face such as Menlo or SF Pro is scored, so its stand-in shows as `fontFamily` drift no edit in Figma can fix: report it as the substitution it is; the real face is the user's to install or map in the desktop app.
   - **`fontAvailable: false` means the measurement describes a substituted typeface**, not the declared one: `fontFamily` is the family the browser drew with, except a named font it couldn't load that the stack lists before any system keyword (such as `-apple-system` or `ui-monospace`), which is recorded as the code asked for it. Say so in the summary, and if Figma also lacks the font name it explicitly ("Figma has no *Inter*; install it or map it") rather than letting it surface as unexplained text drift. storysync cannot install fonts into Figma; that's manual in the desktop app.

   **What snap couldn't measure.** Measure it again first, as Components step 3 says. Only for what still fails, and only in this root-only push (a push through `figma code` builds it as a placeholder instead), read the component source (`.tsx`/`.jsx`, CSS modules, styled-components, inline styles, `cva`/`clsx`/`cn`) and resolve tokens through `npx storysync tokens --json` or the project's own config — never from a memorized class-to-value table, which is wrong for any custom palette.

   **Record every fallback.** Step 6 writes a `source` of `"measured"` or `"inferred"` per variant. A run inspected later must be able to tell them apart without this conversation.

   **Checkpoint before step 5**: every variant has measured styles or an explicit source-derived spec, as Components step 3 says.

   **Label the sets after the build, on every push**, in one `use_figma` call over every set and plain component this push built, by the id step 5 returned: `await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id)`, then split its description into lines, drop the line that starts `storysync:`, add `storysync: ` and the note (`measured from its closed trigger, button[data-slot="dialog-trigger"], not the open dialog`, say) for a set built from a closed trigger or a layout wrapper, and set the description to the lines joined again only if that changed it. Every other line stays, a designer's own included, and a set with no note loses the line an earlier push left, so a set rebuilt once its story renders it open no longer says it was measured from its trigger. Figma returns a description HTML-escaped, `"` as `&quot;` and `&` as `&amp;`, so one written back as it was read gains another escape on every push (a live push left `button[data-slot=&quot;dialog-trigger&quot;]` on Dialog): unescape what you read once (`&amp;`, `&lt;`, `&gt;`, `&quot;`, `&apos;` and `&#39;`) before splitting it, and compare the result with that, so a second run changes nothing. Where `descriptionMarkdown` is not empty, a designer's formatted description, read and write that instead, so their formatting stays, with each of the note's `*`, `_`, `[`, `]`, `<`, `>`, backtick and backslash escaped with a backslash; otherwise read and write `description`. The same call removes Figma's default page, `Page 1`, but only while it is as Figma made it: still named `Page 1`, empty once loaded (`await page.loadAsync()` first, since a page not yet loaded lists no layers, which loads it without switching to it), and not the file's only page. Leave the user on the page they are on: switch to another only if they are on `Page 1`, just before `page.remove()`, and back to it if Figma refuses. Return which it was, `removed`, `kept: it holds layers`, `kept: it is the only page`, `kept: Figma refused to remove it` with Figma's reason, caught rather than thrown so the labels stand, or `none` where the file has no page of that name, and say so in the summary; a page a designer renamed or used is left alone.

4. **Organize the Figma file by Storybook hierarchy.** Create one Figma page per `category`, the whole of it (e.g. `Forms`, `Data Display`, `ui/base`). Components without a category go on a `Components` page. Place each component set on its category's page, named by the last segment of its `title`, the name Storybook's sidebar shows (`Button` for `Forms/Button`, and `Tooltip` for `ui/base/Tooltip` though its `name` is `TooltipContent`), or by its `name` when it has no title. This mirrors the Storybook sidebar so designers find things where they expect them, and keeps apart components that share a name: `ui/base/Button` and `ui/radix/Button` go to the pages `ui/base` and `ui/radix`. Find a category's page ignoring case, spaces, `/`, `-` and `_`, so a page an earlier push named `UI Base` is still the page for `ui/base`. A set an earlier push named by a component's `name`, where that differs, is left as it is: name it in the summary.

5. Write with `use_figma` — find or create the target page, then create the component set on it. The plugin code must embed the measured values from step 3: every variant gets a concrete fill, text color, padding, radius, border, font size and shadow. A call that only references variant names is a bug; refuse it and re-read the snap output. Name each variant from its snap combination: every key, BOOLEAN ones included, as `key=value`, joined with `, ` in the combination's key order. **Update an existing set of the same name rather than creating a duplicate**; a second push must not produce two `Button`s. Look for it anywhere on its page with `page.findAllWithCriteria({ types: ['COMPONENT_SET', 'COMPONENT'] })`, leaving out a set's variants, not only among `page.children`: a designer may have moved it into a section or frame. Find it by its name, and where nothing has the name, by the Storybook id it carries (below), so a set a designer renamed is found while a copy of one, which keeps its id, is not taken for it. Leave out a plain component with no id where you build a set: it is a designer's own. If more than one matches, throw, naming each one's type, name, node id and Storybook id, and ask the user which to update. If the one that matches carries another component's id, throw before changing anything and ask the user whether it is this component's: if so, clear its id in a call of its own and build again; otherwise skip the component.

   **A component with no variant properties is one plain component, not a set.** Its snap combination is empty, so its one variant is named `''`: build it with `figma.createComponent()`, named and placed as its set would be, and restyle it in place on the next push, finding it by name as a set is (`types: ['COMPONENT_SET', 'COMPONENT']`, leaving out a variant inside a set). A push before 0.4.0 built one as a set of one variant named `''`, which Figma renames `=` and marks as broken (`Component set has existing errors`), so reading its properties throws. Never build beside one: if the page has a set of that name where you build a plain component, or a plain component an earlier push built, carrying the component's id, where you build a set (its code has since gained variant properties), throw before changing anything, naming its type and id, and ask the user whether to convert it or to skip the component, saying so in the summary. To convert the old set, in a call of its own, put its one variant where the set is, in the set's parent, and give it the set's name, which keeps every instance of it linked (`const { parent, x, y, name } = set; parent.insertChild(parent.children.indexOf(set), variant); Object.assign(variant, { x, y, name })`), then remove the set if Figma left it behind (`if (!set.removed) set.remove()`); to convert a plain component, combine it into a set of its own where it is (`const { parent, x, y, name } = component; const set = figma.combineAsVariants([component], parent); Object.assign(set, { x, y, name })`), and name the variant from snap's default combination. Then build it again.

   **Put a new set or component below everything on its page**, 100 below the lowest thing there, at the page's left edge, before you create its variants, rather than at `0,0` on top of another; leave one already on the page where it is. A push before 0.4.0 left every set at `0,0`, one on top of another: have each part return `overlaps`, how many other sets and components its set lies on, and where that is not 0, ask the user whether to move it below the rest of its page in a call of its own, and say in the summary which sets overlap and whether they were moved; never move one on your own. **Keep the component's Storybook `id` and `title` on each set or plain component** as shared plugin data, `node.setSharedPluginData('storysync', 'id', id)` and `'title'` (an empty string where it has none), on every push, so the first part can find a renamed set by it, and the audit can pair a set with its component by it.

   **Keep a bound color's alpha.** Figma gives a paint assigned freshly bound to a variable (`figma.variables.setBoundVariableForPaint`) an opacity of 1, so a translucent color bound that way loses its alpha (a live push read Attachment's error border, `#df22254d` bound to `destructive`, back as `#df2225`): assign the bound fills or strokes, then assign them again with each paint's opacity, which Figma keeps once the binding is stored. An effect bound to a color variable takes that variable's alpha, so assign a shadow bound to a variable of another alpha unbound, in its measured color. **If styling a variant throws part way, remove the variants the part created before rethrowing**, so none is left loose on the page, and a retry makes them again. Before changing the text of a label an earlier push made, load the font it has (`figma.loadFontAsync(text.fontName)`), or make a new label where that font won't load.

   End the plugin code by returning the component set's id (`componentSet.id`): step 6 reads the variants back from it, and fixes target it. Build a set in parts of at most 25 variants: `use_figma` takes at most 50,000 characters of code per call, and a variant's measured values run to about a thousand characters, so a large set's code does not fit in one call; a call that runs too long also fails, with `Script exceeded time limit`. Write the values as a table, one entry per variant, leaving out `border` where `borderUniform` is set and `borderRadius` where `borderRadiusUniform` is, which only repeat them, and create the variants in a loop over it. Measure each part's code before sending it: if it passes 45,000 characters, build that set in parts of 15 instead. Build a set of more than 25 variants across calls, in snap's variant order: the first finds the set by name anywhere on its page, or creates it, with the first 25; each later call finds it by id and does the same with the next 25; and every part lays out and checks the whole set as it then stands, so the last leaves it laid out in full. If a call fails with `Script exceeded time limit`, check what it left on the canvas before retrying, and build in smaller parts.

