the columns still line up, a row or column no variant fills at all is left out, and the work grows with the set's variants, not with every combination, which for one property of three values and 20 `BOOLEAN`s passes three million. Make each column as wide as its widest variant and each row as tall as its tallest, 20 apart, so a Drawer's 39 by 24 closed trigger and its 1280 by 197 open panel sit apart, and a variant built 0.01 high keeps a row of its own. A variant whose name is no combination of the properties, one snap does not have, goes in a row of its own below the others, in name order, and so does a second variant of a name: of two variants of a name, the one whose node id sorts first as text stays in the grid, and ties in that row go by id, so every part places them the same. The Button in step 3's snap output — `variant` primary, danger, outline; `size` sm, lg; `disabled` — is three rows, primary, danger and outline, of four columns: sm, sm disabled, lg, lg disabled. **Refuse a set with auto layout.** On a set whose `layoutMode` is not `'NONE'`, auto layout positions the variants itself: setting their x and y does nothing, and the layer order below would flow the last variant into the top-left, Figma's default. So check it before changing anything, and throw `The set "…" has auto layout …`. Ask the user whether to turn auto layout off on the set (`componentSet.layoutMode = 'NONE'`, in a call of its own) and re-run the part, or to leave its layout alone and skip the set, saying in the summary that it was not pushed. Do neither on your own. Figma's layers panel shows a set's last child at the top, so put the variants last to first, keeping the children already in that order and `appendChild`-ing only the rest after them, none when the set is in order already: the panel then reads top-down in the grid's order, row by row, with that extra row last. Grow the set to fit with `resizeWithoutConstraints`, then check that the layers are in that order, that every varia