### Variable binding for a root-only push

When token variable collections exist, bind component properties to variables instead of hardcoding values:
- Fills → bind to the matching variable from the Colors collection
- Padding / spacing → bind to the Spacing collection
- Corner radius → bind to the Radius collection
- Font size → bind to the Typography collection
- Drop shadows → bind to the Shadows collection

A measured value matches a variable when it equals what the variable was set from: its token's `hex` for a color, its `px` for a length. A translucent color, `#rrggbbaa`, matches a variable of its `#rrggbb` too, as Tailwind's `border-destructive/30` is the `destructive` token at 30%. Several variables often share a value: on shadcn/ui, `#171717` is five color tokens, `primary` and `sidebar/primary` among them, and 10px is both the default radius and `lg`. Then bind the one the component's source names for that property. Read the classes the variant renders with (its `cva` variant, or its `className`) and take the token from the utility, named as `tokens` names it, with `/` for each `-`: `bg-primary` is `primary` for the fill, `text-primary-foreground` is `primary/foreground` for the text, `border-input` is `input` for the stroke, and `rounded-lg` is the radius `lg`. The source picks only among the variables of the measured value; the value is still snap's. A class with an opacity modifier, such as `bg-destructive/10` or `border-destructive/30`, names its variable at an opacity: snap measures it translucent, `#df22254d` say, which matches `destructive`'s `#df2225`, so bind it to `destructive` with the paint's opacity at its alpha, assigning the bound paint a second time with that opacity, since the first assignment resets it to 1 (step 5). A shadow's color has no opacity of its own, so bind it only to a variable of its alpha. If you can't read the source, or it names no variable of that value (an arbitrary value like `bg-[#171717]`, or a class set elsewhere), leave the value unbound and list it in the summary, with the variables it matches, rather than pick one. A project that uses Tailwind's default spacing, type or shadow scales has no tokens for them, so those values stay as measured.

This way, when tokens change in code and storysync runs again, updating the variables updates every component.

### Visual accuracy for a root-only push

- Use auto-layout so components resize properly; measured `display`, `flexDirection`, `padding` and `gap` map onto it directly.
- Add a text layer carrying the text the story actually renders — the story's args if they set the content, otherwise the component's default children (e.g. `children = "Button"`). Read it from source if you need to: the rule against reading source is about styles, not labels. Inline components are scored on width, so a different label reads as drift. Style it from the measured `text` values where present, and size it as `text.layout` says: a label snap gives no layout hugs its content, and one it gives a layout is fixed at that width, cut off or wrapped as the browser drew it, so a long title stays inside its frame.

## Audit

Compare the current Figma file against code to find drift in either direction. Use this when asked to "check if Figma is in sync", "audit the design system", or "diff Figma vs code".

If `use_figma` doesn't return plugin return values, fall back to the Figma MCP's read-only tools that Cursor lists for the server, knowing what each covers: `get_metadata` outlines one node's layers (with no node, it lists the pages), and `get_variable_defs` returns only the variables and styles the selected node uses (on the remote server, the node a Figma link points at), so a variable no layer uses never appears. For a complete list, ask the user to export Figma's variables and components to JSON.

1. **Read Figma variables** — call `use_figma` to enumerate all variable collections and their values. Use `figma.variables.getLocalVariableCollectionsAsync()` and `figma.variables.getVariableByIdAsync()` to read each variable's name, resolved type, and value in the collection's first mode, unless the user names a mode, and return each collection's mode names (`coll.modes.map((m) => m.name)`) with the first slice, for the summary to name the modes not compared. Convert COLOR values to hex strings: `#rrggbb`, then the alpha as two more hex digits when it comes to less than `ff`, as tokens writes a translucent color. `use_figma` takes at most 50,000 characters of code and returns at most 20kb per call; the code is small, but a full palette passes 20kb, so read 100 variables per call and return where the next call should start, calling again until none remain. `use_figma` JSON-encodes the string a call returns, so if `JSON.stringify` of it passes 17,000 characters, throw rather than return it, and read fewer at a time.

2. **Read Figma components** — call `use_figma` to enumerate component sets, and components outside any set, a page at a time: the push makes a component with no variant properties a plain component. `use_figma` loads pages as it switches to them, starting each call on the first, and does not support `figma.loadAllPagesAsync()`, so a search from `figma.root` sees only the pages already loaded and misses the sets on the rest. List the pages first (`figma.root.children`, each page's id and name), then make one call per page, issued together: each gets its page with `figma.getNodeByIdAsync`, switches to it once with `await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page)`, and finds its sets and components with `page.findAllWithCriteria({ types: ['COMPONENT_SET', 'COMPONENT'] })`, leaving out a component whose `parent` is a `COMPONENT_SET`, which is a variant. For each, return its `name`, `type`, `nodeId` (`node.id`), and the `storybookId` the push stored on it, `node.getSharedPluginData('storysync', 'id')`, or `null` where that is `''`. A plain component has no variant properties. For a set, read `componentPropertyDefinitions` inside a `try`: a VARIANT property's values are its `variantOptions`, a BOOLEAN's are `true` and `false`, and Figma throws `Component set has existing errors` on a set it flags as errored, so return that set with the `error` and read on, rather than lose the page. Return as many per call as stay under the same size check, and where the next call should start, calling again until none remain; throw only when one alone passes it, so a page of a few hundred icons takes a few calls rather than one per 25.

3. **Extract code tokens** — run storysync:

```bash
npx storysync tokens --json --project .
```

   If its `warnings` say storysync can't read or find part of the Tailwind config, or doesn't follow it, or read no theme from it, code is missing the tokens that part declares. In step 5, report a Figma variable that code lacks as possibly coming from what the warning names, not as orphaned, and name the warning in the report.

4. **Map code components** — run storysync:

```bash
npx storysync map --storybook http://localhost:6006 --json
```

   Pass the `--exclude-props` the push used, if any, or every prop it left out is reported missing from Figma. snap records them as `excludeProps` at the top of `.storysync/snaps/styles.json`, and the push's summary names them. If neither has them, as in a fresh clone, and props come back missing from Figma on components the push made (those with a `storybookId`), ask the user whether the push left those props out before you report them.

   If the user asked about some components only, first run `npx storysync list --storybook http://localhost:6006 --json`: it lists every component's `id`, `name`, `title` and `category` in one call, for step 6 to pair with. Then pass map, with `--components`, only the names in the scope that list has, by `id`, `title`, `name` or the last segment of `title`, ignoring case, rather than map every component, which can take many minutes. map refuses a name Storybook doesn't have, and a name only Figma has is for step 6. If list has none of them, skip map.

5. **Compare tokens** — match Figma variable collections to code token categories (Colors → colors, Spacing → spacing, etc.). For each token:
   - In code but not in Figma → **missing from Figma** (needs sync)
   - In Figma but not in code → **missing from code** (orphaned or manually added)
   - Both exist but values differ → **value mismatch** (show both values)
   - Normalize before comparing: compare a color by its token's `hex` and a spacing, radius or size token by its `px` (rem and `calc()` worked out) where the token has one; otherwise lowercase hex, convert rem→px, strip units for numerics. A translucent color is `#rrggbbaa` on both sides: compare all eight digits. Round numbers to thousandths on both sides.
   - Note what can't be compared, for the summary to name: every mode of a Figma collection but its first; the dark theme, which `tokens` doesn't read, where a `tokens` warning starts `Only the light theme is read`; the tokens a `tokens` warning says it didn't read (`Unresolved:`, `Skipped dynamic value:`, `Not read from @theme`, `Uncategorized:`); and the categories `tokens` has no collection for in a project on Tailwind, whose components use Tailwind's default spacing, type or shadow scale, which `tokens` doesn't extract.

6. **Compare components** — pair each code component with a Figma component from step 2, a set or a plain component. **By ID first**: one whose `storybookId` is a code component's `id` is that component's, whatever its name or page (two with the ID, as a copy of a set has, are told apart by page as below); one whose `storybookId` no code component has is Figma only, and never pairs with another component by name. **Then by name**, for the code components no ID paired and the sets with no `storybookId`: pair each code component with a Figma component set of its set name, case-insensitively: the last segment of its `title`, or its `name` when it has no title, as Components step 4 names sets, or its `name` where no set has that and it is no other component's set name, as an earlier push named them. A name only one code component and one set have is a pair, whatever page the set is on. If two code components share a name (e.g. `Forms/Button` and `Nav/Button`), or two Figma component sets do (on different pages: an archived copy, say, or each category's own `Button`), counting those an ID paired, tell the rest apart by page, even when one is left on each side: Radix's `Button` is still one of two when Base UI's paired by ID, and a `Button` set left on another page may be a designer's own. A code component and a set of its name on its category's page (`Components` when it has none), each the only one of that name on that page on its side, are a pair, comparing page names ignoring case, spaces, `/`, `-` and `_`. Of the copies left, code components alone are code only, and sets alone Figma only. Copies left on both sides, even one of each, can't be told apart (a set on another page may be the component's, moved, or an archived copy beside a component not pushed yet): report the name as ambiguous, and only as ambiguous: compare none of the copies left, and don't also count them as matched, mismatched or missing. Name a component that shares its name by its `title`, and a set by its page and name. A component outside any set with no `storybookId` may be a designer's own, such as an icon named `Calendar`: it pairs only with a code component the sets left code only, of its set name and on its category's page, each the only one there, and never makes a name ambiguous. One left unpaired is not one the push made, since the push stores a `storybookId` on each component it makes: don't list it as Figma only, but count it in the summary as not compared, unless the scope names it. A set with an `error` pairs as any set, but report it as **errored in Figma (can't read its properties)**, by name, page and `nodeId`, and compare nothing in it; don't delete it unless the user asks. When the audit is scoped, pair as above with every component `list` gave, as components with no variant properties, but compare and report only those in scope, and a Figma component paired with none only if the scope names it, by name, page and name, or `storybookId`; a name in the scope that neither `list` nor Figma has is a typo: say so. For each:
   - In code/Storybook but not in Figma → **code only**
   - In Figma but not in code/Storybook → **Figma only**
   - Both exist → compare variant properties: missing props, extra props, missing/extra values.

7. **Report** — present a structured drift report grouped by category/component. Use `+` for missing from Figma, `-` for missing from code, `~` for value mismatch, `?` for ambiguous, `!` for errored in Figma. End with a summary: N tokens matched, N mismatched, N missing. N components matched, N mismatched, N code-only, N Figma-only, N ambiguous, N errored in Figma, and N components outside any set not from the push, not compared. Beside the token counts, say what they cover and what they don't, so `39 matched` isn't read as every token in both themes: `39 matched (colors and radius, light mode only; not compared: the Dark mode of Colors and the code's .dark theme, font/sans and font/mono, which tokens couldn't resolve, and Tailwind's default spacing, type and shadow scales, which have no tokens)`. Say "Figma and code are in sync." only if everything matches and no name is ambiguous or set errored; components outside any set not from the push don't count against it. An ambiguous name was compared on none of its copies, and an errored set on nothing, so either can hide drift: while any is, name them instead, and never say the two are in sync. Say with it what the audit compares: tokens, in the modes it noted, and each component's variant properties, not the styles the push's `verify` scores.

8. **Score how the sets look, when the user asks.** Steps 1 to 7 compare token values and variant properties, not what a set looks like. To audit that too, score the sets as a push's `verify` does, reading Figma and changing nothing in it:

```bash
STORYSYNC_PROGRESS=1 npx storysync snap --storybook http://localhost:6006 --variants all --out .storysync/audit/snaps --json > .storysync/audit-snap-out.json 2> .storysync/audit-snap-progress.log; echo "exit $?" >> .storysync/audit-snap-progress.log
npx storysync figma code --snap .storysync/audit/snaps/styles.json --out .storysync/audit/figma
```

   Run the snap in the background, as Components step 3 does, outside the sandbox, with the components in scope as `--components`, the push's `--exclude-props`, and the push's `--stories`, as `--stories <id>=<storyId>` for each component in scope with the `storyId` the push's `.storysync/snaps/styles.json` records for it, so each is measured from the story the push measured it from (scored from another story, its layers would read as drift that isn't there); where that file isn't there, as in a fresh clone, take them from the push's summary, or ask the user whether the push measured any component from another of its stories. Check that it ended `exit 0`. It writes under `.storysync/audit`, so the push's own snap and readback stay as they are. Then send **only the plan's `read` calls**, as Components step 5 sends calls, saving each response unchanged and continuing each with `npx storysync figma next --plan .storysync/audit/figma/plan.json --seq <its seq>`. Never send its `build`, `images` or `label` calls: they change the file. A read call that throws `Found 0 sets with storysync id …` names a set storysync never pushed, or one on another page: note it, run `figma code` again with that component in `--skip`, and send the read calls again. Then:

```bash
npx storysync verify --plan .storysync/audit/figma/plan.json
```

   It writes the readback to `.storysync/audit/figma-readback.json`. Add to the report, per component: its element fidelity and each difference, by key and node id; elements missing from Figma, and layers for elements snap no longer measures; designer layers; pictures not filled, which score as a wrong picture; the sets an earlier storysync pushed root-only, whose variants carry no element keys, so `verify` reports each variant missing and lists it as `not built by storysync`, which a push with Components step 5 deepens; the sets no read found; and that the element-tree readback doesn't read variable bindings yet, so it can't say how many paints are bound to a variable. Report what it finds; never fix it in the audit.

## Visual accuracy guidelines

- **Measured values from `snap` are ground truth.** They come from a real browser rendering the real component. Where they disagree with your reading of the source, the measurement is right — computed style accounts for the cascade, inherited values, and anything a class name does not reveal.
- **Never invent a value that could be measured.** Guessing that "a primary button is typically blue" produces a library that looks plausible and is wrong, which is worse than one that is visibly incomplete. In a push through `figma code`, a variant snap couldn't measure is built as a placeholder that says so, never styled from source. Only a root-only push, which the user asked for, falls back to the source for one, as its section says, and records it as `inferred`, which `verify --strict-measured` fails on.
- **The calls are the build.** Each layer `figma code` writes, its key, styles and place, comes from snap's measurement, and `verify` scores Figma against that measurement, not against the calls. A call or a layer changed by hand builds or keeps what nobody measured: send the calls as written, and report what they can't build.
- The goal is a Figma library a designer can use directly, not variant scaffolding. The build gives each frame auto layout where Figma's auto layout puts its children where the browser did, so the components resize as the code's do, and binds their colors and radii to the token variables.
